{"causes":["Hesiod's family had a significant impact on his life and work.","The Archaic period saw a shift towards more didactic poetry in Greece.","The Greek concept of the 'agronomos' (farmer) emerged during this time."],"effects":["Works and Days influenced later poets such as Virgil and Dante.","Its themes of nature and agriculture continue to inspire writers today.","The poem's focus on the natural world helped shape Western attitudes towards the environment."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Hesiod","sourceName":"Hesiod","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Works and Days","targetName":"Works and Days","context":"As a key work of the Archaic period, Works and Days provides valuable insights into the lives of early Greeks."},{"sourceSlug":"Zeus","sourceName":"Zeus","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Hesiod's Journey to the Underworld","targetName":"Hesiod's Journey to the Underworld","context":"Hesiod's experiences with his father Zeus are central to the poem."},{"sourceSlug":"Athena","sourceName":"Athena","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Cerberus","targetName":"Cerberus","context":"Hesiod encounters the goddess Athena and the three-headed dog Cerberus on his journey."}],"places":[{"name":"Ancient Greece","role":"Birthplace"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Works and Days","type":"Reference article","slug":"works-and-days"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q667285","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Works_and_Days"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Works and Days contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Works and Days (Greek: Ἔργα καὶ Ἡμέραι, Erga kai Hēmerai) is a didactic poem in dactylic hexameter by the ancient Greek poet Hesiod (fl. c. 700 BCE), generally dated c. 700–650 BCE and, together with the Theogony, the primary source for ancient Greek mythology and the earliest systematic account of the Greek agricultural and moral world. The poem consists of approximately 828 lines addressed to Hesiod's brother Perses, who has cheated Hesiod out of his inheritance through a corrupt legal judgment — the poem combines moral exhortation, mythological narrative, and practical agricultural instruction in a distinctive genre that has no clear precedent in earlier Greek literature.

Works and Days contains several of the most influential narrative passages in the Western mythological tradition: the myth of Prometheus and Pandora (explaining how evil and toil entered the world — Pandora opening the jar/pithos and releasing evils, with Hope alone remaining inside); the myth of the Five Ages of Man (Gold, Silver, Bronze, Heroic, and Iron ages — a moral-historical account of humanity's progressive decline from the Golden Age of Cronus through the present Iron Age of toil and injustice); and the fable of the hawk and the nightingale (one of the earliest Greek fables, illustrating the power of the mighty over the weak). The poem's agricultural calendar (the second half) provides detailed instructions for the farmer's year — when to sow, plough, harvest, prune — and preserves the earliest systematic account of Greek agricultural practice.

Hesiod's Works and Days established the didactic genre of Greek poetry and the concept of the agricultural year as a moral and cosmic framework — the farmer's relationship to the earth, the seasons, and the divine order of Zeus is both practical and theological. The poem's moral framework (justice, dike, as the foundation of human community; toil as the condition of mortal life) provided the earliest Greek systematic ethical thought outside of the Homeric epics.","subjects":["Greek Literature","Ancient Era","Hesiod","Didactic Poetry","Greek Mythology","Agricultural Poetry","Mythology","Archaic Greece"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Works and Days is a significant work of ancient Greek literature that has influenced Western literature and continues to inspire writers today.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
